      "source": "https://www.townofmeggettsc.org/about-us.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "At one point in its history, Meggett was known as the Cabbage Capital of the World.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofmeggettsc.org/about-us.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Chartered in 1905, Meggett became a major Lowcountry freight hub in the 1920s, connecting truck farmers and waterway freight systems.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofmeggettsc.org/about-us.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The town once served as a major distribution point for the second largest oyster and fish cannery in the state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        25
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.townofmeggettsc.org/about-us.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Towles Farmstead in Meggett was listed on the National Register of Historic Places in 1994.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meggett%2C_South_Carolina"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Local farmers once formed the South Carolina Produce Association to better market their primary crops of cabbage and potatoes.",
